Trace:

  1. __initiateWithdraw → _unstakeAndExitPool(...) → delegatecall into LP_LIB, then delegatecall
  ILPLib.initiateWithdraw(account, sharesHeld, exitBalances, withdrawData). Because this is a delegatecall,
  address(this) is the vault, so any token/ETH custody is the vault's.
  2. In BaseLPLib.initiateWithdraw, for each non-zero exitBalances[i] it resolves manager =
  ADDRESS_REGISTRY.getWithdrawRequestManager(address(tokens[i])). Unlike hasPendingWithdrawals and
  tokenizeWithdrawRequest, this loop has no if (address(manager) == address(0)) continue; skip. So if the ETH sentinel
  leg has no registered manager, manager.initiateWithdraw(...) is a high-level external call to address(0): Solidity's
  extcodesize check plus the abi.decode of an empty return buffer reverts the whole transaction. The claimed "request
  booked with nothing behind it" cannot arise in the no-manager case — the tx simply reverts.
  3. If a manager is registered for the ETH sentinel leg, the exploit depends entirely on behaviour of
  IWithdrawRequestManager.initiateWithdraw that is not present in either file shown. The claim asserts the manager will
  happily "book a request denominated in exitBalances[i]" without receiving anything. Every manager implementation must
  acquire the yield token somehow — a transferFrom(msg.sender, ...) (which is exactly why checkApprove precedes the
  call) or a msg.value check for an ETH-denominated manager. For an ETH-sentinel manager, a transferFrom on address(0)
  reverts (call to codeless address), and a value-based manager would revert on msg.value == 0. Nothing in the payload
  shows a manager that credits a request while receiving zero tokens and zero ETH; that is the load-bearing step of the
  claim and it is unverifiable from the code under review. Note also WITHDRAW_TOKEN() is read back in
  finalizeAndRedeemWithdrawRequest, implying managers are token-custodial.
  4. The "double-counting" mechanism is also not what the code does. convertToAssets's escrow branch replaces the normal
  valuation: it returns ILPLib(LP_LIB).getWithdrawRequestValue(account, asset, shares) and does not add the vault's
  residual native-ETH balance, so there is no path where the same ETH is counted twice. Furthermore
  getWithdrawRequestValue loops over all TOKENS() and require(hasRequest) for each leg — including any leg whose
  registry entry is address(0), where the external call itself reverts. So the escrow pricing branch only succeeds when
  every leg genuinely has a booked request; a half-booked ETH leg makes valuation revert, not over-value.
  5. The derived slice (CurveConvex2Token) is a truncated, non-compiling fragment (duplicate TOKENS() definition) and
  contains none of the ETH/WETH handling in CurveConvexLib — the appendix imports ETH_ADDRESS, ALT_ETH_ADDRESS, WETH,
  which strongly suggests the exit path wraps/unwraps ETH in the lib, but that code is not shown. The claim needs
  behaviour from code absent from both files.

  Bottom line: the one concrete control-flow fact in the shown code (no zero-manager skip in initiateWithdraw, so the
  ETH leg either hits a real custodial manager that must pull value or reverts on a call to address(0)), plus the fact
  that the escrow valuation branch returns the request value instead of, not in addition to, the vault's holdings,
  defeats the stated exploit as described. The remainder rests on unshown manager code — tie-break applies.

